/**
 * @defgroup MIDILAR_Specification MIDI-Domain Specification
 * @brief Invariants every MIDILAR type will follow, written before the code.
 *
 * Each invariant gets a stable identifier (`SPEC-MIDI-1`, ...) referenced from
 * the unit tests that prove it, as in MCC's music-domain specification. This
 * page starts with the scope and the design rules; the protocol invariants are
 * written in phase 1 of `ACTION_PLAN.md`, before any MIDI type exists.
 *
 * @section MIDILARSpecScope Scope
 *
 * - MIDILAR owns MIDI 1.0 protocol data: status and data bytes, channels,
 *   note numbers, velocities, controllers, system common, real-time, MTC and
 *   SysEx; parsing a byte stream into messages; routing; and devices that
 *   transform message streams.
 * - Music theory (pitches, spelling, scales, chords, keys) belongs to MCC.
 *   MIDILAR converts between MIDI note numbers and `MCC::Pitch` with explicit
 *   range checks, and spells incoming notes through `MCC::Key`.
 * - General utilities (buffers, callbacks, time, scheduling, flash data)
 *   belong to Foundation. Signal processing belongs to the future DSPCore.
 *
 * @section MIDILARSpecRules Design rules
 *
 * - SPEC-RT-1: Real-time paths (parsing, routing, devices) never allocate
 *   memory dynamically and never throw exceptions.
 * - SPEC-RT-2: Value types are trivially copyable, with size budgets checked
 *   at compile time for every target (`src/MIDILAR.cpp`).
 * - SPEC-RT-3: Every value type has one canonical invalid value; invalid input
 *   produces it instead of undefined behavior.
 * - SPEC-RT-4: Variable-length data (SysEx) is read from and written to
 *   caller-provided buffers.
 * - SPEC-RT-5: Constant tables live in program memory on AVR through
 *   `FOUNDATION_FLASH`.
 */
